<section class="hero"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="eyebrow">Value chains · rare-earth magnets</div>
  <h1>$title</h1>
  <p class="deck">China’s share of output is higher at separation and magnet production than at the mine. EU customs records show how direct imports changed around the 2025 licensing episode. A six-month stress test puts a planned European expansion in scale and identifies the evidence needed to estimate automotive deliveries.</p>
  <p class="byline">Research briefing · revised 24 November 2026</p>
</div></section>
<article>


<aside class="panel" id="scope-addendum" aria-labelledby="scope-addendum-heading">
<h2 id="scope-addendum-heading">Scope clarification · 24 November 2026</h2>
<p>This briefing asks how large the EU’s direct-import exposure is and how much of that historical import mass Narva Phase 1B could nominally offset. The question below about the binding stage for qualified automotive magnets defines follow-on research.</p>
<p><strong>Modelled maximum nominal mass offset.</strong> The <strong>$gap_offset</strong> ratio compares up to $block_ceiling tonnes of proposed future magnet block capacity for six months with $gross_gap tonnes of China-origin CN 85051110 import mass recorded in April–September 2024. The comparison prorates the annual increment and the minimum offshore-wind allocation. The import basket includes finished magnets and articles for magnetisation across different end uses. The nominal ratio precedes adjustments for machining and coating yields, grade compatibility, qualification and customer allocations. The future capacity is compared with a fixed historical benchmark. Phase 1A’s existing nameplate capacity is outside the calculation because the scenario measures only the proposed Phase 1B increment. <a href="$comext">Eurostat Comext</a> · <a href="$expansion">Neo project disclosure</a> · <a href="$blocks">Neo earnings call, November 2026</a>.</p>
<p><a href="#origin-addendum">See the detailed origin concentration and the 2025 import comparison</a>.</p>
</aside>

<h2>Where production is concentrated</h2>
<p>Which stage would restrict qualified magnet deliveries to EU automotive customers if Chinese supply were interrupted for six months? The International Energy Agency (IEA) has already established the concentration of downstream production and identified magnet manufacturing and metallisation as major obstacles to global diversification. This study checks the scale and timing of direct EU import exposure and asks how much a named European expansion could replace under stated assumptions. <a href="$iea">IEA, Rare Earth Elements</a>.</p>

<figure class="panel">
  <figcaption><strong>The physical route to a sintered magnet</strong> · China’s share of global output where the IEA reports a comparable estimate for 2024</figcaption>
  <ol class="chain stages">
$stage_map
  </ol>
  <p class="note">The nodes show successive production steps. The market shares use different denominators. The diagram depicts processing order rather than measured transfers between sites. <a href="$iea">IEA</a> · <a href="$doe">US Department of Energy supply-chain assessment</a>.</p>
</figure>

<p>Neodymium (Nd) and praseodymium (Pr) are the main rare-earth inputs in many neodymium–iron–boron (NdFeB) magnets. Some grades use dysprosium (Dy) or terbium (Tb) to preserve coercivity at higher temperatures. The requirement depends on design, grade and process. Manufacturers can reduce heavy rare-earth use in some grades, and changing the magnet or motor design requires the customer to validate it. <a href="$doe">US Department of Energy assessment (2022), sections 2.1–2.2 and table 2</a>.</p>

<h3>How to read the mining and refining figures</h3>
<p>The IEA’s 2024 mining estimate covers Nd, Pr, Dy and Tb. The USGS’s broader mine tally includes lanthanides and yttrium and excludes most scandium. In its 2026 summaries, USGS estimates China’s output at 270,000 tonnes of rare-earth-oxide equivalent in both years, against rounded world totals of 380,000 in 2024 and 390,000 in 2025. Those ratios are about 71% and 69%, respectively. The Atlas’s earlier BGS panel uses its own broader series and country estimates. The IEA's July 2026 announcement of its critical-minerals outlook puts the top supplier at 85% of all rare-earth refining in 2025, a broader scope than the April report's magnet rare earths, so the two figures are not a trend. Keep that vintage separate from the April report’s 2024 estimate and from the Atlas validation snapshot. <a href="$usgs">USGS mine table</a> · <a href="$bgs">BGS World Mineral Statistics</a> · <a href="$outlook">IEA 2026 outlook</a> · <a href="../casestudies#magnet-audit-note">Atlas validation note</a>.</p>

<h2>Direct EU imports fell midyear and recovered</h2>
<p>Eurostat records gross EU-27 imports from outside the EU by declared origin. CN 85051110 covers metal magnets and articles for magnetisation containing neodymium, praseodymium, dysprosium or samarium. The line includes products beyond automotive sintered NdFeB, including samarium-containing magnets, and excludes magnets classified as part of imported motors or other goods. Customs origin gives the origin of the declared product. It gives no upstream provenance for the rare earths. <a href="$comext">Eurostat Comext</a>.</p>
