<section id="origin-addendum" aria-labelledby="origin-addendum-heading">
<h2 id="origin-addendum-heading">Import concentration by declared origin · 24 November 2026</h2>
<p>The country and territory records reconcile to Eurostat’s official extra-EU total, including a small mass without a named origin. The named-origin HHI sums squared shares of the named mass. The all-import range uses the official total and allows the unnamed mass to be dispersed or wholly assigned to the largest origin. Both measures show higher concentration in 2025 than in 2024. They describe declared origins of direct imports of CN 85051110. <a href="out/comext_origins.json">Eurostat origin extract</a> · <a href="https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat/documents/3859598/23532096/KS-01-26-020-EN-N.pdf/db644144-6a01-6dc3-217f-b1e65c4ccf8e?t=1776338334640&amp;version=1.0">Eurostat geonomenclature</a>.</p>
<div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Detailed import concentration estimates">
<table>
<caption>EU-27 gross extra-EU imports · net mass · HHI on a zero-to-one scale</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Year</th><th scope="col">Tonnes without a named origin</th><th scope="col">Named-origin HHI</th><th scope="col">All-import HHI range</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
$origin_rows
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>In April–September 2025 other origins provided no aggregate offset in the customs basket. Their recorded net mass fell by about $other_origin_drop tonnes year on year while China-origin mass fell as well. Which factors explain the absence of an aggregate offset? Testing limits on capacity, product compatibility, contracts, overall demand or classification requires more detailed product, supplier and buyer records. This episode gives historical context and a plausibility check for timing and scale. Validation of the hypothetical interruption and expansion would require observed access to qualified supply, inventory drawdowns and unfilled orders. <a href="out/comext_audit.json">Monthly Eurostat inputs</a> · <a href="../export-controls">Atlas comparison with ferrite</a>.</p>
</section>

<h2>How much could a planned expansion replace?</h2>
<p><span class="conf estimate">Modelled</span> Use April–September 2024 as a six-month import reference. Remove all China-origin entries in the basket and hold other recorded origins fixed. The gross volume to replace is <strong>$gross_gap tonnes</strong>. This screen counts direct import mass only. The historical licensing episode covered selected products, and this scenario assumes a complete interruption of the customs basket. It assigns no credit to stocks, existing EU production, embedded magnets or new imports, and holds flows of rare-earth oxides and alloys outside the screen. <a href="out/magnet_chain.json">Scenario calculations</a> · <a href="../shock-methods">Atlas shock methods</a>.</p>
<p>Neo’s planned Phase 1B at Narva would add up to 3,000 tonnes a year of permanent-magnet capacity (described as block capacity on the company's November call), with a third allocated to EU offshore wind under the proposed project. Prorating nameplate and the wind allocation across the year leaves up to <strong>$block_ceiling tonnes of blocks</strong> for other applications. Under full effective availability and a one-for-one replacement assumption, that could offset at most <strong>$gap_offset</strong> of the historical direct-import gap. Actual replacement requires qualified grades, machining and coating yield, usable feedstocks, time to deliver and uncommitted slots. The ceiling assigns all non-wind volume to this exercise and allows that its actual contribution could be zero. Automotive demand coverage requires a verified grade-level demand denominator. <a href="$expansion">Neo, October EU grant announcement</a> · <a href="$blocks">November capacity basis</a>.</p>
