<h2>What determines whether a stage binds?</h2>
<div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Operational definitions for concentration, dependency, vulnerability and binding constraints">
<table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Measure</th><th scope="col">How to test it</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Concentration</th><td>Country shares or HHI within a defined production stage or customs basket. State the denominator and year.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Dependency</th><td>Share of the target market’s required grade sourced from a disrupted route, including embedded and upstream inputs where traceable.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Vulnerability</th><td>Demand left unserved during a named disruption after considering accessible stocks and independent, qualified supply that can deliver in time.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Binding constraint</th><td>A stage whose relaxation raises feasible deliveries with the rest held fixed. When stages tie or depend on each other, test a joint relaxation too.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
</div>
<div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Evidence needed to compare supply options by stage">
<table><caption>Capacity and substitution questions alongside the output shares above</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Stage</th><th scope="col">What an alternative route must demonstrate</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
$stage_rows
</tbody></table>
</div>
<p>For a single qualified route, express each stage’s usable output as finished-magnet equivalents after material and yield conversions. Deliveries through that route are capped by its smallest stage limit. Grade-compatible scrap can enter later as recycled alloy if the process and customer accept it. Finished-magnet stock and qualified direct imports enter at the delivery stage. In a multi-route model, allocate each stock draw, processing slot and shipment once. Plant-level throughput and buyer allocations are needed to put numbers on those limits. <a href="$doe">US Department of Energy supply-chain assessment</a> · <a href="out/magnet_chain.json">Facility observations and scenario inputs</a>.</p>

<div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Intervention comparison">
<table><caption>How investment options affect the same disruption model</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Option</th><th scope="col">When it can reduce the gap</th><th scope="col">Cost and time evidence</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">More separation</th><td>Raises throughput if separated feed is limiting and qualified metal, alloy and finishing slots are available.</td><td>Project-specific cost, ramp and available tonnes need verification.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">More metal or alloy</th><td>Raises throughput if conversion is limiting and compatible separated feed and downstream slots are available.</td><td>Project-specific cost, ramp and available tonnes need verification.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">More sintered-magnet capacity</th><td>Raises throughput only where compatible feed, finishing steps and customer approvals support incremental deliveries. The Narva screen gives an optimistic scale for one planned increment.</td><td>Operator gives a bundled estimate. Stand-alone cost and first qualified delivery timing are unresolved.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Recycle production scrap</th><td>Can bridge constrained upstream stages if traceable scrap yields compatible alloy or powder and the magnet maker has usable slots.</td><td>Recoverable yield, delivered cost, qualification and time need confirmation.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Expand linked stages</th><td>Can relieve bottlenecks that bind together. The proposed Estonian project combines separation, metallisation and magnet manufacturing.</td><td>Operator estimates about €400 million for the package and two to three years to complete, subject to investment and grant conditions. <a href="$expansion">October project disclosure</a>.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
</div>

<h2>Facility evidence and open questions</h2>
<p>These plant records illustrate different milestones. A commissioning run establishes capability at a stated stage. A commercial shipment establishes qualification for an initial programme. Estimating available supply requires operating output, product-grade coverage, independent feed and uncommitted allocations for the six-month window.</p>
